# Application configuration.
#
# Every tunable the firmware has is declared here and consumed through
# src/config.hpp, so no module reads a Kconfig symbol directly and no behaviour
# is changed by editing a source file.

menu "BLE concentrator"

# Uplink transport ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The concentrator ships in two variants that differ only in how collected
# readings leave the device. Choosing here is what selects the one backend
# compiled under src/hal/link/, and what pulls in the Zephyr subsystem that
# backend needs, so prj.conf carries neither LoRa nor networking symbols.

choice APP_LINK_BACKEND
	prompt "Uplink transport"
	default APP_LINK_LORA
	help
	  Which hal::link backend the firmware is built with. Nothing above
	  hal/link changes with this choice.

config APP_LINK_LORA
	bool "LoRaWAN"
	select SPI
	select LORA
	select LORA_SX127X
	select LORAWAN
	# The stack stores its MAC context in settings, which is what carries the
	# DevNonce over a reboot. From LoRaWAN 1.0.4 an OTAA join must present a
	# DevNonce higher than the last one the join server saw for that DevEUI, so
	# a device that restarts at zero has its join refused as a replay - a join
	# that used to work and now silently does not. These were in prj.conf,
	# where they were also switched on for builds that have no LoRa stack to
	# store anything.
	select FLASH
	select FLASH_MAP
	select NVS
	select SETTINGS
	# Not SETTINGS_NVS or LORAWAN_NVM_SETTINGS: both are choice symbols,
	# and select has no effect on those. Neither is needed anyway. The
	# settings backend choice already carries `default SETTINGS_NVS if
	# NVS`, and the LoRaWAN NVM choice carries `default
	# LORAWAN_NVM_SETTINGS if SETTINGS`, so selecting NVS and SETTINGS
	# above is what picks both.
	help
	  Uplinks go out over LoRaWAN through an SX127x radio described in the
	  board overlay under the lora0 alias.

config APP_LINK_TCP
	bool "TCP"
	select NETWORKING
	select NET_IPV4
	select NET_TCP
	select NET_SOCKETS
	select NET_MGMT
	select NET_MGMT_EVENT
	help
	  Uplinks go out over a TCP connection to a fixed server. The network
	  interface comes from the board: an integrated MAC on a Nucleo, or an
	  SPI Ethernet controller such as a W5500 on a board without one.

config APP_LINK_WIFI
	bool "Wi-Fi"
	select NETWORKING
	select NET_IPV4
	select NET_TCP
	select NET_SOCKETS
	select NET_MGMT
	select NET_MGMT_EVENT
	select WIFI
	select NET_L2_WIFI_MGMT
	help
	  Uplinks go out over a TCP connection carried by Wi-Fi. The socket is
	  the same one the TCP backend uses - the two share
	  hal/link/socket/ - and the only difference is associating with an
	  access point first.

	  Built for the ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1. Two things about that part are
	  worth knowing before choosing this over the wired variant:

	  Wi-Fi and Bluetooth share one 2.4 GHz radio. The scanner goes deaf
	  while Wi-Fi transmits, which is a silicon-level compromise against
	  the rule in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md section 4 that nothing may delay
	  the radio. The nRF52840 plus a wired interface does not have it.

	  Espressif's radio drivers need a system heap and declare how much
	  through HEAP_MEM_POOL_ADD_SIZE_*: 51200 bytes for Wi-Fi and another
	  25600 for the Bluetooth controller, which on this SoC is a vendor
	  blob rather than Zephyr's own. Zephyr provisions exactly the sum.
	  Nothing in this firmware allocates either way; see prj.conf.

endchoice

if APP_LINK_LORA

config APP_LINK_LORA_REGION_US915
	bool "Use the US915 regional parameters"
	default y
	select LORAWAN_REGION_US915
	help
	  The only region implemented today. EU868 is not a matter of flipping
	  this symbol: it has no sub-bands, a single-word channel mask, an
	  enforced duty cycle and a different payload table. See docs/GATEWAYS.md.

config APP_LINK_LORA_SUBBAND
	int "US915 frequency sub-band the gateway uses"
	default 2
	range 1 8
	depends on APP_LINK_LORA_REGION_US915
	help
	  US915 has eight sub-bands and a gateway listens on exactly one.
	  Sub-band n covers the eight 125 kHz channels starting at (n-1)*8, plus
	  the 500 kHz channel 63+n. A device left to hunt all 72 channels takes a
	  very long time to join or never joins, and the symptom looks like an RF
	  or wiring fault rather than a configuration one.

	  Read this off the gateway: on a MultiTech Conduit it is
	  Setup -> LoRa -> Frequency Sub-Band. The unit this firmware is tested
	  against uses 2, which is also the common default.

config APP_LINK_LORA_DEV_EUI_FROM_HWINFO
	bool "Derive the DevEUI from the SoC's factory identifier"
	default y
	select HWINFO
	help
	  The nRF52840's factory identifier is eight bytes and unique per part,
	  which is exactly a DevEUI. Deriving it means a unit can be flashed and
	  powered on without being registered first, and no two units can
	  accidentally ship with the same identity.

	  This works because the gateway accepts any DevEUI presenting the right
	  AppEUI/AppKey pair. Against a join server with per-device credentials,
	  turn this off and set the DevEUI it was registered with.

config APP_LINK_LORA_MAX_UPLINKS_PER_DISPATCH
	int "Uplinks sent per dispatch cycle"
	default 3
	range 1 8
	help
	  How many LoRaWAN transmissions one dispatch cycle may make. Every
	  fragment of an uplink is a separate transmission, and a burst of
	  them back to back from one node is what gets a device throttled by
	  the network server.

	  Three, with the 900 s period above, is roughly 1.2 s of airtime
	  every 15 minutes at DR3 - about 0.13%, which no regional rule or
	  fair-use policy objects to. It reports about 27 devices per cycle at
	  25 bytes a record, so a room of 20 goes out in one pass.

	  Raising it further is not obviously wrong, but measure the airtime
	  first: at DR0 a single packet holds the channel for roughly a
	  quarter of a second, and the arithmetic that makes 3 comfortable at
	  DR3 is not the same one at DR0.

	  Devices that do not fit in a cycle are not dropped. Nothing is
	  acknowledged until it is sent, so they are still pending and the
	  next cycle leads with them. The cost of a low number is latency, not
	  data.

endif # APP_LINK_LORA

if APP_LINK_TCP

config APP_LINK_TCP_USE_DHCP
	bool "Obtain the IP address over DHCP"
	default n
	select NET_DHCPV4
	help
	  Off by default on purpose. With a static address a network that is not
	  there fails at connect() and the state machine retries, which is a
	  state the firmware already handles. With DHCP the device instead has
	  no address at all, and "the network is not up yet" becomes
	  indistinguishable from "the network is gone".

config APP_LINK_TCP_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "Milliseconds to wait for the TCP connection to establish"
	default 5000
	range 100 60000

config APP_LINK_TCP_MAX_FRAGMENT
	int "Largest uplink fragment handed to the transport, in bytes"
	default 242
	range 32 242
	help
	  TCP imposes no such limit, but reporting one keeps svc::comms
	  fragmenting exactly as it does on LoRaWAN, so the uplink wire format
	  and the fragmentation path are identical on both variants and only one
	  of them has to be tested. 242 is the LoRaWAN ceiling, so a fragment
	  built here is always one a LoRa build could also have sent.

endif # APP_LINK_TCP

if APP_LINK_WIFI

config APP_LINK_WIFI_USE_DHCP
	bool "Obtain the IP address over DHCP"
	default y
	select NET_DHCPV4
	help
	  On by default here, unlike the wired backend. An access point is
	  almost always handing out addresses already, and a Wi-Fi deployment
	  is the one where nobody wants to reserve a static address per
	  concentrator by hand.

config APP_LINK_WIFI_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "Milliseconds to wait for the TCP connection to establish"
	default 5000
	range 100 60000

config APP_LINK_WIFI_MAX_FRAGMENT
	int "Largest uplink fragment handed to the transport, in bytes"
	default 242
	range 32 242
	help
	  Wi-Fi imposes no such limit, but reporting one keeps svc::comms
	  fragmenting exactly as it does on LoRaWAN, so the uplink wire format
	  and the fragmentation path are identical on all three variants and
	  only one of them has to be tested.

endif # APP_LINK_WIFI

menu "Per-installation values"

# Addresses, credentials and endpoints, for every transport, defined
# unconditionally and on purpose.
#
# These used to live inside the `if APP_LINK_<transport>` blocks, which meant
# a symbol only existed in the build that used it - and a single gitignored
# prj_local.conf holding one installation's LoRa keys and its Wi-Fi password
# then failed every build but one, because Zephyr treats "assigned a value to
# a symbol whose dependencies are unmet" as an error.
#
# The rule is now: the transport choice gates *behaviour*, never *values*. One
# prj_local.conf describes one installation and builds every variant of it.
# Nothing is compiled in that the build does not use: src/config.hpp still
# reads each group under its own #if, and the linker never sees the rest.

config APP_LINK_LORA_JOIN_EUI
	string "JoinEUI / AppEUI, 16 hex characters"
	default "0102030405060708"
	help
	  Identifies the application on the join server. Not a secret.

config APP_LINK_LORA_APP_KEY
	string "AppKey, 32 hex characters"
	default "000102030405060708090a0b0c0d0e0f"
	help
	  The root key, from which the session keys are derived. It never leaves
	  the device and never travels over the air.

	  The default here is deliberately not a real key. Override it in a
	  prj_local.conf that .gitignore excludes, so a clone of this repository
	  builds without carrying anyone's credentials.

config APP_LINK_LORA_DEV_EUI
	string "DevEUI, 16 hex characters"
	default "0000000000000000"
	depends on !APP_LINK_LORA_DEV_EUI_FROM_HWINFO

config APP_LINK_TCP_LOCAL_IP
	string "Static IP address of this concentrator"
	default "192.168.1.50"
	depends on !APP_LINK_TCP_USE_DHCP

config APP_LINK_TCP_NETMASK
	string "Static netmask"
	default "255.255.255.0"
	depends on !APP_LINK_TCP_USE_DHCP

config APP_LINK_TCP_GATEWAY
	string "Static default gateway"
	default "192.168.1.1"
	depends on !APP_LINK_TCP_USE_DHCP

config APP_LINK_TCP_SERVER_ADDR
	string "IPv4 address of the uplink server"
	default "192.168.1.10"
	help
	  Dotted-quad only. Resolving a name would make every uplink depend on a
	  DNS server being reachable, which is one more thing to fail between
	  this device and its data.

config APP_LINK_TCP_SERVER_PORT
	int "TCP port of the uplink server"
	default 5000
	range 1 65535

config APP_LINK_WIFI_SSID
	string "SSID of the access point"
	default "changeme"
	help
	  Up to 32 characters. Override it from a gitignored prj_local.conf,
	  the same place the LoRa keys live, so a clone of this repository does
	  not carry anyone's network.

config APP_LINK_WIFI_PSK
	string "Pre-shared key of the access point"
	default ""
	help
	  8 to 64 characters for WPA2-PSK. An empty string means an open
	  network, which is the only case where empty is a valid setting
	  rather than a forgotten one.

	  This is a credential. Put the real one in prj_local.conf.

config APP_LINK_WIFI_LOCAL_IP
	string "Static IP address of this concentrator"
	default "192.168.1.50"
	depends on !APP_LINK_WIFI_USE_DHCP

config APP_LINK_WIFI_NETMASK
	string "Static netmask"
	default "255.255.255.0"
	depends on !APP_LINK_WIFI_USE_DHCP

config APP_LINK_WIFI_GATEWAY
	string "Static default gateway"
	default "192.168.1.1"
	depends on !APP_LINK_WIFI_USE_DHCP

config APP_LINK_WIFI_SERVER_ADDR
	string "IPv4 address of the uplink server"
	default "192.168.1.10"
	help
	  Dotted-quad only. Resolving a name would make every uplink depend on
	  a DNS server being reachable, which is one more thing to fail between
	  this device and its data.

config APP_LINK_WIFI_SERVER_PORT
	int "TCP port of the uplink server"
	default 5000
	range 1 65535

endmenu # Per-installation values

config APP_MAX_DEVICES
	int "Maximum endpoint devices tracked at once"
	default 100
	range 1 500
	help
	  Capacity of the device table. A new device arriving at a full table
	  evicts the least recently seen entry, and the eviction is counted and
	  reported in the uplink. This is the hard limit on how many sensors one
	  concentrator can report from a room.

config APP_ADV_REPORT_POOL_SIZE
	int "Depth of the raw advertising report pool"
	default 32
	range 4 256
	help
	  How many advertising reports can be waiting between the BLE callback
	  and the acquisition thread. This is the burst tolerance: reports that
	  arrive when the pool is full are dropped and counted, never queued at
	  the cost of blocking the Bluetooth stack.

config APP_EXPECTED_COMPANY_ID
	hex "Company identifier accepted in endpoint advertisements"
	default 0x05AA
	help
	  Advertisements carrying any other company id are discarded. This is a
	  filter, not an identity: every endpoint of the product line shares it.

	  Must match the value the endpoint firmware advertises, which is
	  `.company_id` in base_platform_baremetal_ble's src/app/app.c. The
	  endpoint writes it low byte first, and this filter compares the value
	  after a little-endian read, so configure it as it reads there: 0x05AA,
	  not 0xAA05.

config APP_DISPATCH_PERIOD_S
	int "Seconds between uplink dispatches"
	# 900 is the exception, not the fallback. It exists because of LoRa
	# airtime and nothing else, so it is conditional on LoRa and every
	# other transport - including one added later - gets 30. Written the
	# other way round, with 900 as the last default, the Wi-Fi build
	# silently inherited a 15 minute period.
	default 900 if APP_LINK_LORA
	default 30
	range 5 86400
	help
	  How often the collected readings are sent. The default differs by
	  transport because the two products differ, not because the code
	  does.

	  LoRa, 900 s: industrial monitoring. Nobody needs to know within a
	  minute that a motor started, and it is the only rate that fits in
	  the airtime a node is entitled to.

	  TCP, 30 s: building and city sensing, where "is this machine in use
	  right now" is a question someone is waiting on. There is no airtime
	  to ration on a wire.

	  Seconds rather than minutes because 30 s does not round to a useful
	  number of minutes, and a unit that cannot express a valid setting is
	  the wrong unit.

config APP_HEARTBEAT_AFTER_CYCLES
	int "Quiet dispatch cycles before a heartbeat is sent"
	default 4
	range 0 255
	help
	  Only devices whose reading has not reached the network yet are sent,
	  so a room where nothing changes produces no uplink at all. From the
	  far end that is indistinguishable from a dead concentrator.

	  After this many consecutive cycles with nothing to report, the device
	  sends a header with no records and the HEARTBEAT flag set. At the
	  default period of 15 minutes, 4 cycles means silence is never longer
	  than an hour.

	  Set to 0 to disable heartbeats entirely. Only do that if something
	  else on the network already proves the device is alive.

config APP_DEVICE_STALE_AFTER_S
	int "Seconds after which a silent device is considered stale"
	default 3600
	range 60 86400
	help
	  A device that has not advertised for this long is left out of the
	  uplink. It stays in the table as an eviction candidate. An uplink
	  listing a device should mean it was heard from recently.

config APP_CONCENTRATOR_ID
	hex "Identifier of this concentrator"
	default 0x00000001
	help
	  Carried in every uplink header so the receiving side can tell which
	  concentrator reported.

config APP_REPORT_BOOT
	bool "Flag the first uplink after a restart"
	default y
	help
	  Sets a flag in the first uplink sent after boot. It tells the
	  receiving side that the device table started empty, so a gap in a
	  device's history is a restart rather than an absence. Without it, a
	  restart has to be inferred, which fails if nobody was listening when
	  it happened.

module = APP
module-str = Application
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
